From 03826b8460f33cf0e1246051006f562347f4eb46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Michaud <jmichaud@igalia.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 10:52:48 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 07/17] [mya] List a snapshot's memory regions, with their
 permissions and page details Need the bug URL (OOPS!).

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

Corpse::Region could only describe the one region containing an address, on
Darwin, with its resident and dirty page counts. The heap walk and mya's
memory commands need the whole map.

Region gains:
- Region::all(task): every region, by address. On Darwin through
  mach_vm_region_recurse, descending into submaps; on Linux from
  /proc/<pid>/maps. Bounded by maxRegionCount.
- Region::allWithPageCounts(pid) on Linux, from /proc/<pid>/smaps, and
  findContaining(pid, address) on Linux, built on it (it returned nullopt).
- Permissions, and on Linux the kernel's name for the region (a path, or a
  label like [stack]).
- On Darwin, the swapped, reusable and dirty page counts, the VM user tag,
  whether the region is private and whether it is file-backed, and
  pageDispositions() (mach_vm_page_range_query), which the footprint
  accounting later in this series uses.
- residentParts(): the parts of a region whose pages are in memory or
  swapped, from /proc/<pid>/pagemap on Linux (the whole region on Darwin
  when it has any resident page). A walk of every writable word reads only
  these: a large reservation is mostly untouched.
- findContaining(regions, address) and readableEnd(regions, address) over a
  sorted list. readableEnd follows adjacent readable regions, since on Linux
  an image's .bss runs on from its last file-backed page into an anonymous
  mapping.

Snapshot::regions() lists and caches a snapshot's regions. readProcFile reads
a whole /proc/<pid>/ file on Linux.

The Region suite now runs on Linux too. A page that was only read maps the
shared zero page, which Linux does not count as resident, so there the
resident count is checked to lie between the written and the accessed pages.

